              "{\"alternativeHeadline\":\"China’s economy off to a bad start to 2019, analysts say\",\"articleBody\":\"Li Chao, an analyst with brokerage house Huatai Securities said that the indicators were gloomy, and expected the picture to remain so for the foreseeable future. “That means the downward pressure on the Chinese economy is still significant,” he wrote in a note. China’s manufacturing shrank again in January, as fears of further economic slowdown mount “Companies are continuing to cut their stock proactively, a move that might not change until the fourth quarter of 2019, meaning weaker support for the gross domestic product,” Li added, commenting on the rebound in stock levels found in the manufacturing index. Marcel Thieliant, senior economist at Capital Economics, warned that the data may be inaccurate, saying that “we are wary of putting too much weight on the official PMIs given that they have provided false signals in the past”. Another metric, the Caixin manufacturing PMI, will be released on Friday and should help provide a fuller picture. “While the official manufacturing PMI did not weaken any further in January, it still suggests that the economy lost momentum at the start of the year,” Thieliant said. Economists at the Japanese investment bank Nomura suggested that the weak export sales figures among the PMI data point to the end of front-loading. Front-loading was a strategy used by many companies as a way of avoiding the US tariffs on Chinese goods. It involves shipping cargoes earlier than they are required, so that they arrived in the US before the tariffs were enforceable. The first raft of US tariffs were implemented in July. This means the Chinese economy will slow further still over the course of 2019, Nomura’s analysts said. “[The economy is] dragged by synchronised shocks of the ongoing credit downcycle, the end of the front-loaded exports, the cooling property sector, and the end of the replacement cycle of consumer durables,” they wrote. The picture is markedly worse for small and medium sized companies, which experienced a rougher start to 2019 than China’s larger companies, official data shows. The growth in non-manufacturing PMI, meanwhile, has been attributed to seasonal issues, with Chinese shoppers spending more money in the run up to the holiday period “Boosts from consumer spending during the Lunar New Year holiday just emerged,” said Zhao Heqing, senior statistician of the NBS. The holiday effect and so-called “consumption upgrade” have boosted telecoms, banks, tourism, postal services, and transport, Zhao said. He added that these will wear off, as cold weather and the return of migrant workers to rural areas for the Lunar New Year starts to hit consumption.\",\"articleSection\":\"China Economy\",\"author\":{\"name\":\"Orange Wang\",\"sameAs\":\"https://www.scmp.com/author/orange-wang\"},\"dateCreated\":\"2019-01-31T13:01:40+08:00\",\"dateModified\":\"2019-01-31T22:47:48+08:00\",\"datePublished\":\"2019-01-31T13:01:40+08:00\",\"genre\":\"Economy\",\"hasPart\":{\"isAccessibleForFree\":\"False\"},\"headline\":\"China economy ‘losing momentum’ as first major data of 2019 paints gloomy picture\",\"inLanguage\":\"en-GB\",\"isAccessibleForFree\":\"False\",\"locationCreated\":{\"name\":\"Hong Kong\"},\"publisher\":{\"name\":\"South China Morning Post\"}}"
